{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bug was using `int(p / 100 * n)` as a 0-based index. That is one rank too high for typical percentiles (e.g. p50 of 1..10 gave 6 instead of 5) and at `p=100` produced index `n`, which is out of range.\n\nThe fix uses the **nearest-rank** rule: 1-based rank `ceil(p / 100 * n)`, then index `rank - 1`, clamped to `[0, n - 1]` so `p=0` is the minimum and `p=100` is the maximum.\n\n```15:17:repo/stats.py\n    rank = math.ceil(p / 100 * n)\n    idx = max(0, min(n - 1, rank - 1))\n    return ordered[idx]\n```\n\n`python3 -m pytest tests/visible -q` — 2 passed."}]},"session_id":"50e4d1c0-45db-41ab-bfc3-f8070e12ef28"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":68136,"duration_api_ms":68136,"is_error":false,"result":"Inspecting the percentile implementation and tests.\n\n\nUsing `ceil(p/100 * n) - 1` for nearest-rank (with clamping). Current code uses `int(p/100 * n)`, which overshoots by one.\n\n\nThe bug was using `int(p / 100 * n)` as a 0-based index. That is one rank too high for typical percentiles (e.g. p50 of 1..10 gave 6 instead of 5) and at `p=100` produced index `n`, which is out of range.\n\nThe fix uses the **nearest-rank** rule: 1-based rank `ceil(p / 100 * n)`, then index `rank - 1`, clamped to `[0, n - 1]` so `p=0` is the minimum and `p=100` is the maximum.\n\n```15:17:repo/stats.py\n    rank = math.ceil(p / 100 * n)\n    idx = max(0, min(n - 1, rank - 1))\n    return ordered[idx]\n```\n\n`python3 -m pytest tests/visible -q` — 2 passed.","session_id":"50e4d1c0-45db-41ab-bfc3-f8070e12ef28","request_id":"20f8a762-d858-4d85-a748-fd7aede8af97","usage":{"inputTokens":11125,"outputTokens":1595,"cacheReadTokens":73849,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
